<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>GG2.Host</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>gg2-server</AssemblyName>
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>false</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
    <TieredPGO>true</TieredPGO>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\GG2.Server\GG2.Server.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ⚠️ So CliCoverageTests can call Program.Parse. That test is the gate this
    project did not have: it asserts every settable ServerOptions property is
    reachable from a flag, or is named as deliberately absent. Seven were
    reachable only from C# — implemented, wired to real behaviour, and
    unsettable by anyone running the binary — and nothing went red.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="GG2.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The built-in maps (22 shipped plus the port-authored gg_debug2) travel WITH
    the server, so `dotnet publish` produces a directory that serves a round on a
    machine with no Gang-Garrison-2 checkout.
    ⚠️ Without this the repo-root walk in MapDirectory.Resolve still finds them
    from bin/, so `dotnet run` works and a PUBLISHED server does not — which is
    the failure that only shows up on the deployment nobody tests.
    458 KB, and Link keeps them in a maps/ subdirectory rather than loose.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\..\maps\*.png" Link="maps\%(Filename)%(Extension)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <!--
      The duel map pool (docs/DUEL.md), in its own directory so a plain server's
      maps/ and every census over it are unchanged. A duel server points its
      maps and botnav options at maps/duel and nav/duel.
    -->
    <Content Include="..\..\maps\duel\*.png" Link="maps\duel\%(Filename)%(Extension)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <!-- MPL-2.0 requires the license to travel with the covered files, and the maps are Gang Garrison 2's. -->
    <Content Include="..\..\LICENSE" Link="LICENSE" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The bot nav graphs and the manifest that says which map each was built from.
    They travel with the server exactly as the maps above do.

    ⛔ These used to be OPT IN behind `-p:PublishNav=true`, on the grounds that
    they were 34 MB. That was a working-tree number: gzipping the container made
    the same graphs 1.11 MB — less than the maps beside them — and git had been
    storing ~1.1 MB of them all along, because hex of ds_grid_write doubles
    compresses about 32:1. The flag was deleted rather than kept, because what it
    bought was a DEFAULT deployment whose bots fight and never walk, and nothing
    goes red when that happens. NavCache.CompressedExtension has the
    measurements; docs/DEPLOY.md has the history.

    ⚠️ Include the whole directory, not "*.txt.gz". An operator's own graphs land
    here as .txt, and manifest.tsv is neither — a glob per container is how one
    of them silently stops shipping.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\..\nav\*"
             Link="nav\%(Filename)%(Extension)"
             CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest"
             CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="..\..\nav\duel\*"
             Link="nav\duel\%(Filename)%(Extension)"
             CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest"
             CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The shipped policy checkpoints, for the same reason as the two above: the
    command README.md and docs/BOTS.md both print names a checkpoint as
    `checkpoints/tier0_combat4_s2.npz`, and a relative path resolves
    against the working directory. Without this it works from the repo — where
    the file happens to be one level up from bin/ — and fails on a published
    deployment, which is the failure mode the maps comment above describes.

    ⚠️ 160 KB, and unlike maps/ and nav/ there is NO fallback: a missing map
    directory is searched for and a missing nav graph is generated, but
    PolicyNetwork.Load on a path that is not there is a refusal with the path in
    it. Which is the right failure — it just has to be one nobody meets by
    publishing.

    ⚠️ The whole directory, README.md included, for the reason the nav glob
    gives: what ships is a directory, and a per-extension glob is how one file in
    it silently stops travelling. checkpoints/README.md is where the numbers
    behind these weights are written down, and it is worth more beside them on a
    server than it is in the repository alone.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\..\checkpoints\*"
             Link="checkpoints\%(Filename)%(Extension)"
             CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest"
             CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
